Hi Hackaday,

the interesting hack in Solidon3D 0.2.2 is not “AI makes a model.” It is a
hard capability boundary around an optional language model controlling a
desktop geometry system.

Menus, shortcuts and chat all request the same registered, schema-checked
operations. The language model may choose an operation and propose parameter
values, but deterministic code computes the geometry. A chat proposal is one
transaction and one undo. Generated source is never executed, and project
files carry operation recipes and values rather than executable code.

This is now testable in practical workflows. A recognised hole in an imported
mesh can be resized on its existing axis through the same operation from the
menu or chat. Selected history steps can become a reusable parametric
component, still represented only by registered operations. Cloud and local
model settings explicitly state which data leaves the machine, while all
non-chat modelling remains available offline.

The released application runs on Windows, macOS and Linux:
https://solidon3d.de/en/

If this is tip-worthy, I can provide the operation schema, a minimal project
file, the two equivalent calls and a screen recording showing that both paths
produce the same geometry and undo as one transaction.

